㊥ (what the look shows) As an inbox, the raw open ports are mostly noise. In my own session, which I can check line by line, the kernel counts 24 open acts, and about 20 of them were answered or done:
- the phone1 tunnel was fixed;
- the spec was fetched;
- "did you get any results back?" was answered;
- "what is milestone 4?" was answered;
- the claude-16 bell was sent and replied to;
- the recorder was restored.

So "open port" currently means "the kernel didn't link an answer to it", not "still owed".

㊢ (what is genuinely open in that list) Four items, which I'd also have named by hand:
- running FUTON in a debugger (your idea, not started);
- merging with M-象-2000;
- the claude-14 replay;
- the backfill delegated to codex-70, which is correctly open because it's waiting on codex-70.

㊢ (the overall numbers) 1,857 open, split three ways:

| source | open |
|---|---|
| operator fragments | 822 |
| agent-to-agent bells | 681 |
| agent replies | 354 |

473 are over a week old. By kind, 144 are `constrain`, which are standing rules rather than to-dos. If my session is typical, most of the 822 are answered asks whose answers weren't linked.

🈖 (why this matters beyond the inbox) It changes step 3 of the plan. claude-16's first proposed measure for the claude-14 replay was "open ports at session end", and that measure would currently mostly count unlinked answers. The replay's measures have to use signals we trust, or check the ports by hand on a sample first.

㊭ (two ways forward)
1. **Report the false-open rate to the 象/kernel side,** with my session as a labelled test case: 24 open, about 20 answered, each listed. Improving how answers are linked is their work, not something to re-implement in -40°.
2. **Build the -40° inbox from signals that are explicit,** meanwhile:
   - open sorries and unapplied proposals;
   - delegations still waiting on a bellback, for example the codex-70 job;
   - 🈸 questions to you that you haven't answered yet. My marked 🈸 paragraphs are classical and easy to detect; whether you answered is the hard part.
   - Raw ports stay available but aren't the headline.

🈸 (one decision) Shall I do both, sending the test case to the 象 side and reshaping the view around explicit signals, and then go on to step 2, crystallisation?

㊥ (backfill) It's done and holds up under my own check. The turn layer is restored: evidence and ports exist for every analysed turn since 10-06, except 7 records with no readable acts.

㊬ (verified independently, with my own random sample rather than codex-70's)
- **The job:** done in work mode, 62 tool calls.
- **The commit:** `5e654230` adds only the addendum to its write-up.
- **My sample:** 5 random analysed records per day for 10-09, 10-10 and 10-11. All 15 have their `e-xiang-turn-…` evidence on :7073 (HTTP 200) and ports on the record.
- **Day totals:** ports are present on 88 of 93, 115 of 117 and 30 of 30 records. That's 7 without, which matches codex-70's 7 residuals exactly.

㊢ (codex-70's numbers)
- 243 records replayed, oldest first;
- 235 evidence entries newly written and 8 quiet duplicates, with 0 failures;
- ports set on 236;
- the first operator turn after the fix, `claude-11-turn-96`, carries the JVM marker;
- no restart or reload.

㊟ (the 7 leftovers) Those turns have readings with no recognised acts, and the live `compute-ports!` refuses them ("No acts for turn"). codex-70 calls it a separate small fix: anchoring an empty turn's timestamp. It isn't urgent, so I've left it unassigned unless you want it done.

🈖 (useful for minus40) Live records since 10-09 carry the service's own ports again, so the follower's computed ports can now be compared against the live service for the outage period too. That will be part of whatever we do with the open view.

🈰 (still waiting on you, from my last message) Shall I send the false-open test case (my session: 24 open, about 20 answered) to the 象 side, reshape the -40° view around explicit open items, and then move on to crystallisation?

